        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Work outside in, cheapest first, and retest after each step. Clean the gutters and extend the downspouts six feet. Regrade so the soil falls away from the house. Dig out and cover the window wells. Seal the rim joist with rigid foam. Vent the dryer properly, cap the sump pit, insulate the cold pipes. Add an interior fan to keep air moving across cold walls.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A large number of basements reading in the high 50s and low 60s land below 50 percent somewhere in that list and never need a machine. If yours does not, you will at least be buying a smaller unit with a lower duty cycle and a longer life, because every source you removed is load the machine no longer has to carry. That is the case for doing this work first rather than instead.
          </p>
